----------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nAny sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contains three points of \\(S\\), those three points must be collinear.  \nA direct check of all \\(\\binom{6}{3}=20\\) triples shows that the only collinear triples are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese three lines are respectively \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\), and \\(x+y=4\\); each has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\) and therefore is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nHence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S\\); it can contain at most two. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf three distinct lines cover all points of \\(S\\) and exactly two of them are sunny, then the remaining (non\u2011sunny) line must contain at least two points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most two points, so the two sunny lines together cover at most four points of \\(S\\).  \nSince \\(|S|=6\\), at least two points are left uncovered and must lie on the third line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nThe non\u2011sunny lines that contain at least two points of \\(S\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&y=1,\\qquad x=1,\\qquad x+y=4 &&(\\text{each contains three points}),\\\\\n&x=2,\\qquad x+y=3 &&(\\text{each contains two points}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=c\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=c\\)), or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nScanning the six points of \\(S\\) we obtain:\n\n- Horizontal lines: \\(y=1\\) passes through \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\); \\(y=2\\) and \\(y=3\\) each contain a single point.\n- Vertical lines: \\(x=1\\) passes through \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); \\(x=2\\) passes through \\((2,1),(2,2)\\); \\(x=3\\) contains one point.\n- Slope \\(-1\\) lines: \\(x+y=2\\) contains one point; \\(x+y=3\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\); \\(x+y=4\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nNo other line of these three families meets two points of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \nFor each line listed in Lemma\u202f3, the set of points of \\(S\\) not lying on that line cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe treat the five possibilities separately.\n\n1. **\\(y=1\\).**  \n   Remaining points: \\(\\{(1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\}\\).  \n   Any pair among them has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), or \\(-1\\); all are non\u2011sunny.  \n   Hence at least three sunny lines would be needed.\n\n2. **\\(x=1\\).**  \n   Remaining points: \\(\\{(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \n   Again each pair has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\); no sunny line can contain two of them.\n\n3. **\\(x+y=4\\).**  \n   Remaining points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   The three possible pairs all have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\).\n\n4. **\\(x=2\\).**  \n   Remaining points: \\(\\{(1,1),(3,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\}\\).  \n   The only sunny pair among these four is \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n   The other two points, \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\), lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\); any line through either of them and any other remaining point has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\).  \n   Consequently two sunny lines cannot cover all four points.\n\n5. **\\(x+y=3\\).**  \n   Remaining points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   The only sunny pair is \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n   The points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) have slope \\(-1\\) between them, and each of them forms a non\u2011sunny line with any of the other remaining points.  \n   Hence two sunny lines are insufficient.\n\nIn every case the leftover points cannot be covered by two sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exist three distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) with exactly two of them sunny.  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 the non\u2011sunny line must contain at least two points of \\(S\\); by Lemma\u202f3 it must be one of the five lines listed there.  \nLemma\u202f4 shows that for each of those possibilities the remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines, contradicting the assumption.\n\nTherefore no such configuration exists.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Impossible}}\n\\]"
