ally, they think that the diagonal line y=x also covers all points with b = floor((n+1)/2) and a = floor((n+1)/2). But that only covers the single point (c,c). The other points with b = c and a ≠ c are not covered. So dropping that horizontal line leaves uncovered points. So the construction fails.\n\nThus the solution's construction for k=1 is invalid. However, maybe there is a different construction for k=1 that works? Let's think: We need n lines total, exactly one sunny. Could we use n-1 vertical lines (x=1,...,x=n) plus one diagonal line y=x? That would also be n+1 lines. Could we use a mix of vertical and horizontal lines to reduce count? The total number of lines must be n. So we need to cover all points with n lines, exactly one of which is sunny. The points are arranged in a triangular grid of size n. The total number of points is n(n+1)/2. With n lines, each line can cover many points. The horizontal lines each cover up to n points (for b fixed). Similarly vertical lines each cover up to n points (for a fixed). Diagonal lines (slope -1) also cover many points. The sunny line can be any other slope. So perhaps there is a construction for k=1 that uses n-1 non-sunny lines but not all of them are horizontal; maybe some are vertical or diagonal. But we need exactly n lines total. So we need to find a set of n-1 non-sunny lines that together with one sunny line cover all points. Is that possible? Let's explore.\n\nWe need to cover the triangular region S_n = {(a,b) ∈ ℕ^2 : a+b ≤ n+1}. Let's denote the points by coordinates (i,j) where i≥1, j≥1, i+j ≤ n+1. The shape is a right triangle with legs of length n (including endpoints). The total number of points is n(n+1)/2.\n\nWe have n lines total. Each line can be horizontal (y = constant), vertical (x = constant), diagonal (x+y = constant), or any other slope (sunny). The non-sunny lines are only of the three types. The sunny line can be any other slope.\n\nWe need to find all possible k such that there exists a c