o find a configuration manually: For n=3, we need to cover points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). The horizontal lines y=1, y=2, y=3 each cover a row. The vertical lines x=1, x=2, x=3 each cover a column. The diagonal lines x+y=2,3,4. But we need exactly 3 lines. If we have 2 sunny lines, they could be e.g., line through (1,1) and (2,2) (slope 1) is not allowed because slope 1 is allowed? Wait, sunny lines are not parallel to x-axis (slope 0), y-axis (vertical), or line x+y=0 (slope -1). So slope 1 is allowed (sunny). So line y=x passes through (1,1),(2,2). It also passes through (3,3) which is not in our set. So line y=x covers (1,1),(2,2). It does not cover (3,1) or (1,2) etc. So we need other lines to cover the rest. Could we have a vertical line x=1 (non-sunny) covering (1,1),(1,2),(1,3). Then we have line y=x covering (2,2). We still need to cover (2,1),(3,1). Could we have a line through (2,1) and (3,1) which is horizontal y=1 (non-sunny). But we already used one non-sunny line (vertical). We need exactly 2 sunny lines, so we can have at most 1 non-sunny line. So that fails. Could we have a sunny line covering (2,1) and (3,1)? That would be a line through (2,1) and (3,1) which is horizontal, slope 0, not allowed as sunny. So cannot. Could we have a sunny line covering (2,1) and (3,1) via some other line? No, they are collinear only on horizontal line. So we need a non-sunny line for that. That would be second non-sunny line, violating k=2. So maybe k=2 impossible for n=3. That matches solution.\n\nNow, could k=4 be possible for larger n? The solution's induction suggests not, because you can reduce the problem by using three non-sunny lines (horizontal y=1, vertical x=1, diagonal x+y=n+1) to reduce to n-2 case with same number of sunny lines. So any solution for n with k sunny lines can be reduced to a solution for n-2 with same k. Conversely, you can extend a solution for n-2 to n by adding those three lines. So the set of possible k for n is the same as