ve non-sunny line be horizontal y=1. That covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1). Then we need to cover (1,2),(1,3),(2,2) with two sunny lines. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,2) and (2,2)? That line would have slope (2-2)/(2-1) = 0, horizontal, not sunny. So can't. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,2) and (1,3)? That's vertical line x=1, not sunny. So can't. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,2) and (3,1)? That's slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, which is not 0,∞,-1, so it's sunny. But (3,1) is already covered by y=1. That's okay. So we could have a sunny line L1 passing through (1,2) and (3,1). That line is sunny. Then we need to cover (1,3) and (2,2). Could we have a sunny line L2 passing through (1,3) and (2,2)? Slope (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, which is not allowed (slope -1). So not sunny. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,3) and (2,2) and maybe also (3,1)? That line would be (1,3),(2,2),(3,1) which is line x+y=4, slope -1, not sunny. So can't.\n\nAlternatively, non-sunny line could be vertical x=1. That covers (1,1),(1,2),(1,3). Then we need to cover (2,1),(2,2),(3,1) with two sunny lines. Could we have a sunny line covering (2,1) and (3,1)? That's horizontal y=1, not sunny. Could we have a sunny line covering (2,1) and (2,2)? That's vertical x=2, not sunny. Could we have a sunny line covering (2,1) and (3,1)? Not sunny. So we need to use two sunny lines each covering maybe one or two points but not both in same row/column/diagonal. Let's try: L1 passes through (2,1) and (3,1) is horizontal, not allowed. L1 passes through (2,1) and (2,2) vertical, not allowed. L1 passes through (2,1) and (3,1) horizontal, not allowed. L1 passes through (2,1) and (3,1) not allowed. L1 passes through (2,1) and (1,3)? slope (3-1)/(1-2) = 2/(-1) = -2, which is not 0,∞,-1, so sunny. So L1 could be line through (2,1) and (1,3). Then L2 must cover (2,2) and (3,1) maybe? Slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. Could L2 cover (2,2) and (3,1)? That's slope -1, not allowed. Could L2 cover (2,2) and (