S_4\\) are covered as follows  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{c|c}\n     \\text{line} & \\text{points of }S_4\\text{ on it}\\\\ \\hline\n     L_1 & (1,1),\\;(2,2)\\\\\n     L_2 & (1,3),\\;(2,1)\\\\\n     L_3 & (1,2),\\;(3,1)\\\\\n     D   & (1,4),\\;(2,3),\\;(3,2),\\;(4,1)\n     \\end{array}\n     \\]\n\n     *Induction step.*  \n     Assume for some \\(n\\ge3\\) we have a covering of \\(S_n\\) consisting of three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) and \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  Translate every line by the vector \\((1,1)\\); i.e. replace a line with equation \\(ax+by=c\\) by the line \\(ax+by=c+a+b\\).  The three sunny lines stay sunny and remain distinct, and the \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines stay non\u2011sunny.  The translated family covers exactly  \n\n     \\[\n     S_n'=\\{(a+1,b+1)\\mid (a,b)\\in S_n\\}\n         =\\{(a,b)\\in S_{\\,n+2}\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     Add the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  They cover all points of \\(S_{\\,n+2}\\) with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).  Consequently the \\(n+2\\) lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\{x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{translated }L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\cup\\{\\text{translated non\u2011sunny lines}\\}\n     \\]\n\n     cover \\(S_{\\,n+2}\\) and contain exactly three sunny lines.  Starting from the bases \\(n=3,4\\) the induction yields a covering with three sunny lines for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Use the same \u201cuncovered\u2011region\u2019\u2019 idea.  With two sunny lines we have \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  By replacing any diagonal non\u2011sunny line with a horizontal or vertical one we may assume the non\u2011sunny lines consist of \\(s\\) horizontals \\(y=b_i\\) and \\(t\\) verticals \\(x=a_j\\) with \\(s+t=n-2\\).  The points not covered by these \\(n-2\\) lines are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\notin\\{a_j\\},\\;b\\notin\\{b_i\\}\\}\n     =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge s+1,\\;b\\ge t+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is isomorphic to \\(S_2\\) and therefore consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  Any line through two of these points is horizontal, vertical or diagonal, i.e. non\u2011sunny; hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of them.  With only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points of \\(U\\), leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  Thus \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\).**  \n   Let a covering contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  As before we may assume the non\u2011sunny lines are \\(s\\) horizontals \\(y=b_i\\) and \\(t\\) verticals \\(x=a_j\\) with \\(s+t=n-k\\).  The uncovered region is  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge s+1,\\;b\\ge t+1\\}\n     =\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines must cover all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n   **Lemma 1.** *For any sunny line \\(L\\) and any integer \\(m\\ge3\\) one has*  \n   \\[\n   |L\\cap S_m|\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  A sunny line meets the triangle \\(S_m\\) in a segment whose endpoints lie on the two legs \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  If the endpoints are \\((1,u)\\) and \\((v,1)\\) with \\(u,v\\ge1\\), then the vector between them is \\((v-1,1-u)\\).  The number of integer points on the segment equals \\(\\gcd(v-1,u-1)+1\\).  Because the line is not of slope \\(-1\\) we have \\(v\\neq u\\); writing \\(v-1=d\\) and \\(u-1=rd\\) with \\(\\gcd(d,rd)=d\\) gives \\(\\gcd(v-1,u-1)=d\\).  The condition \\(v+u\\le m+1\\) yields \\(d\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{m-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).  Consequently \\(|L\\cap S_m|\\le d+1\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\). \u220e\n\n   **Lemma 2.** *If a sunny line attains the bound of Lemma\u202f1, then its slope is either \\(1\\), \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\); equivalently the line is one of*  \n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+(m+1),\\qquad x+2y=m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  Equality in Lemma\u202f1 forces \\(\\gcd(v-1,u-1)=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{m-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).  Hence \\(v-1\\) and \\(u-1\\) are either equal (giving slope \\(-1\\), forbidden) or one is twice the other.  The three possibilities \\((v-1,u-1)=(d,d)\\), \\((d,2d)\\) or \\((2d,d)\\) correspond respectively to slopes \\(1\\), \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\).  The line must also pass through the points \\((1,u)\\) and \\((v,1)\\), which yields the three explicit equations above. \u220e\n\n   Returning to the uncovered set \\(S_k\\): it contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.  By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can cover at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points, so we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality holds only when \\(k\\) is odd; for even \\(k\\) the left\u2011hand side is strictly smaller.  Moreover, when \\(k\\) is odd the bound forces every sunny line to attain the maximum of Lemma\u202f1, and by Lemma\u202f2 each of them must be one of the three special lines listed there.  Thus at most three sunny lines can cover \\(S_k\\).  Consequently \\(k\\le3\\).  Since we have already exhibited coverings for \\(k=0,1,3\\), the only admissible values are \\(0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion** \u2013 The set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]"
